---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick } from '../../content/site';
import { faqPageLd } from '../../content/readme';

const title = 'CLAUDE.md vs memory: when to use hippo | hippo-memory';
const description =
  'CLAUDE.md holds rules you write; auto memory holds notes Claude writes itself. What each loads, where it lives, and where a lesson store like hippo fits.';
const docs = 'https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory';

// Only this page's answers get FAQPage markup here; the README question is marked up once, on /faq/.
const qa = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between CLAUDE.md and auto memory?',
    a: "`CLAUDE.md` files hold instructions you write; auto memory holds notes Claude writes itself from your corrections and preferences. Both load at the start of every conversation, as context rather than enforced configuration. `CLAUDE.md` can sit at user, project and local level, and the project file can be checked in; auto memory stays on the machine, in `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/` (Claude Code's memory docs, checked 2026-09-28).",
  },
  {
    q: "How much of Claude Code's auto memory loads each session?",
    a: "The first 200 lines of `MEMORY.md` or the first 25KB, whichever comes first, per Claude Code's memory docs (checked 2026-09-28). `MEMORY.md` is the index of auto memory, with one topic file per memory beside it.",
  },
];
const cards = [
  {
    name: 'CLAUDE.md',
    body: 'Rules you write for every session: build and test commands, code style, things never to do. The project file can be checked in, so your team shares it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Auto memory',
    body: "Notes Claude writes itself from your corrections and preferences. On by default, loaded at the start of each conversation, and kept on this machine.",
  },
  {
    name: 'hippo',
    body: 'Lessons that pile up as you work: what failed, what fixed it, which facts changed. Recalled when they match the task, marked wrong or superseded when they stop being true, and shared with Codex, Cursor and your other agents.',
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="CLAUDE.md vs memory" published="2026-09-28" schema={[faqPageLd(qa)]}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro eyebrow="Comparison">
      <Fragment slot="title">CLAUDE.md vs memory: <span class="text-gradient">what goes where</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        If you are working out how to deal with a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> file properly, most of the answer is deciding
        what belongs in it and what belongs somewhere else. Claude Code has two memories of its own: the
        <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files you write and the auto memory it writes. Hippo adds a third kind, a store of lessons
        that are recalled when they match the task.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Claude Code's two memories (checked 2026-09-28)">
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files hold instructions you write. They can sit at four levels: managed policy, user
          (<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>), project (<code>./CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>./.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>) and
          local (<code>./CLAUDE.local.md</code>). Every file found is added to context; none overrides another.
        </li>
        <li>
          Auto memory holds notes Claude writes itself from your corrections and preferences: a <code>MEMORY.md</code>
          index and one topic file per memory, in <code>~/.claude/projects/&lt;project&gt;/memory/</code>. It is on by
          default and stays on the machine.
        </li>
        <li>
          Both load at the start of every conversation, as context rather than enforced configuration. For auto memory,
          that is the first 200 lines of <code>MEMORY.md</code> or the first 25KB, whichever comes first.
        </li>
        <li>
          When two rules contradict each other, Claude may pick one arbitrarily. <code>/doctor prompt-audit</code> looks
          for contradictions and stale instructions and proposes edits.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Source: Claude Code's
        <a class="link" href={docs} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">memory docs<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What goes where" alt>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        {cards.map((c) => (
          <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-zinc-100">{c.name}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">{c.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Bringing CLAUDE.md notes into hippo">
      <Cmd lines={['# preview first, then import', 'hippo import --claude CLAUDE.md --dry-run', 'hippo import --claude CLAUDE.md']} />
      <p>
        The import skips hippo's own block and any duplicates. After that, <code>hippo sleep</code> at the end of each
        session also imports new entries from the project's Claude Code auto memory, so its notes reach the store your
        other agents read. <code>hippo init</code> adds a short block to <code>CLAUDE.md</code> telling Claude
        to run <code>hippo context --auto</code> when a session starts, and leaves the rest of the file alone. The
        <a class="link" href="/claude-code/">Claude Code page</a> covers the hooks.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq items={[...qa, ...faqPick(['Do I still need CLAUDE.md?'])]} heading="CLAUDE.md questions" moreHref="/faq/" />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
